{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1670 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ca419c03358bffcf8fd8963a15ffe1702ec69d40028f5dced0e3943d66301c9f" :edb/db-before-t 1669 :edb/db-after-t 1670 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534982 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:08:50.040Z" 13194139534982 true] [17592186175889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324601 13194139534982 true] [17592186175901 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324608 13194139534982 true] [17592186175901 1020 #edb/ref 17592186324615 13194139534982 true] [17592186176101 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534982 true] [17592186176101 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $r = 3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 false] [17592186176101 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $r=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 true] [17592186176101 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/aa435c0ad4b4482c85a05af32afbbb7819b330a4292762ec66bad30a6ed31a44.png)" 13194139534982 true] [17592186176104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534982 true] [17592186176104 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $z = r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 false] [17592186176104 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $z=r$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 true] [17592186176104 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=r\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\frac{r}{r}=1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the half-cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/69978d3f1026cf83019924c44d66f0e510ce8c84b35d10c77c270b87809b30aa.png)" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324602 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1089 "q-104231" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =5$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1113 #uuid "fdc63001-5d2c-5b7d-9d01-a89ed92bf4ec" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324601 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $5$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =5$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/a7e2f3f2ad24c353bc3dc63d82971b4888ccffc198479456beddd7fea185bd07.png)" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324603 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324604 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324605 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324606 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324607 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1041 "selection" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324604 13194139534982 true] [17592186324602 1110 #uuid "665e5a57-89fa-5faa-aa00-3080921957ec" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324603 1034 "A circular sector" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324603 1111 #uuid "fe35e88a-ac21-5d09-864b-5b7dac52b4d0" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324604 1034 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324604 1111 #uuid "b48bf848-c75a-51ad-8c63-85ecd27be008" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324605 1034 "A sphere" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324605 1111 #uuid "4a202b4d-e096-5801-8a9d-f3af2131cbc5" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324606 1034 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324606 1111 #uuid "1e528de0-2028-5c8e-8b82-bed4e9a9c539" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324607 1034 "A half-cone" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324607 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324607 1111 #uuid "0718333a-9b84-52de-bb98-61017fd197fc" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324609 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1089 "q-104240" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1100 "What surface is defined by the equation ${z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1113 #uuid "f262fe7e-0560-56f7-ac9c-ac81b1d50660" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324608 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals half the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis. In other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\frac{r}{z}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{{r}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}}}=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the tangent of this angle can be a positive or negative number, the $z$-coordinate can also be positive or negative.\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the cone shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/7c8d0cefa5eb8da76f6cba59570670e4078c278c801aeb12344dce3183733149.png)" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324610 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324611 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324612 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324613 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324614 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1041 "selection" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324610 13194139534982 true] [17592186324609 1110 #uuid "0ac7644e-8d52-5ea5-9144-2f0e04f902b2" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324610 1034 "A cone" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324610 1111 #uuid "de0c19f0-67cd-5fd4-9de9-e3b139a0472e" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324611 1034 "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324611 1111 #uuid "2ca12f86-20c8-5786-8a71-ae3f0f5beffe" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324612 1034 "A sphere" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324612 1111 #uuid "f95fb564-0626-5e59-9757-094c07ae709c" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324613 1034 "A plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324613 1111 #uuid "81e7d79e-6d0d-50ba-a9e2-44176d52d194" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324614 1034 "A circular cylinder" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324614 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534982 true] [17592186324614 1111 #uuid "3b188870-9ae9-58f0-a27d-f54e32e59912" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1017 #edb/ref 17592186324616 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1089 "q-104244" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/ee2764f5cf85f39107e458f347b048cc24227a55fae012b12f6cda4801cdfe78.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1113 #uuid "6332f9d4-cc02-5d38-8ecf-e05dcc290462" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324615 1145 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a circular cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis. \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- From the diagram, the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nThe tangent of this angle equals the ratio between the distance $r$ from the point to the $z$-axis and the distance $z$ from the point to the $xy$-plane. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{z} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ \\frac{r}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ z & =r\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in its domain.\n\nFinally, recall that $r\\ge 0.$ Since we wish to include both half-cones, we need to include $z=-r\\sqrt{3}$ as well. We can combine the two equations by squaring both sides.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is ${z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}.$" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324617 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324618 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324619 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324620 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1000 #edb/ref 17592186324621 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1041 "selection" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1104 #edb/ref 17592186324617 13194139534982 true] [17592186324616 1110 #uuid "4984c0be-9153-5d1f-891f-21429c65a30e" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324617 1034 "{z}^{2}=3{r}^{2}" 13194139534982 true] [17592186324617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534982 true] [17592186324617 1111 #uuid "abd168b4-e300-5c48-9370-4e183d0c38